\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless otherwise noted, main-text results use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B). Domain-level $\gamma_d$ estimates are available at both $J=13$ (Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}) and $J=16$ (Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma}); we report $J=16$ values in the text.

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

\paragraph{Item filtering improves calibration.}
RRF-guided item filtering improves IRT calibration over random item subsets: on a random 2000-item MMLU subset ($J=13$), vanilla 2PL achieves $\tau = 0.46$; on the 300-item RRF-selected subset, $\tau = 0.73$ ($\Delta\tau = 0.27$). Exclusion sensitivity analysis (removing 0--50\% of top-exposure items) yields $\tau$ from 0.46 to 0.60 with no monotonic trend, and $\gamma$ remains non-significant at all exclusion rates.

\paragraph{RRF vs.\ single-signal ablation.}
S1-only item selection causes IRT convergence failure ($\hat{R} = 1.53$, ESS$=$7) due to exposure variance compression: top-300 S1 items concentrate in a narrow score band, which creates collinearity between $\gamma \cdot e_{ij}$ and difficulty parameters. Multi-signal RRF fusion preserves broader exposure distributions necessary for $\gamma$ identifiability. Random 300-item subsets ($J=16$, 5 seeds) yield $\tau = 0.554 \pm 0.048$ and MAE$= 0.024 \pm 0.012$, which confirms that RRF's advantage ($\tau = 0.731$, $\Delta\tau = 0.177$) reflects informative item selection, not subset size.

\paragraph{Aggregate contamination effects are negligible.}
Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares calibration on 300 RRF-filtered items ($J=16$). Raw accuracy achieves the lowest MAE (MMLU 0.006) and outperforms all correction methods including vanilla 2PL and ContamCal. This occurs because SFT-injected contamination shifts aggregate accuracy by only ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp across dosage levels (a model-free observation: raw accuracy difference between d000 and d100), an effect smaller than the estimation noise introduced by any correction procedure. ARC exhibits the same pattern (raw MAE 0.007) but IRT model fit is marginal (PPP$=$0.003); full ARC results are in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}. The IRT model differentiates ability across scales ($\Delta\theta = 0.50$, 95\% CI $[0.43, 0.57]$ pooled; Appendix~\ref{sec:theta_separation}), which confirms that $\theta$ estimation functions correctly; the calibration gap arises because correction overshoots a near-zero true effect. Credible interval coverage is benchmark-dependent: MMLU over-covers (95\% CI: 100\%) due to insufficient likelihood-to-prior ratio at $J=16$; ARC under-covers (95\% CI: 90\%) due to structural misfit (PPP$=$0.003). See Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity} for root-cause analysis.

\paragraph{Diagnostic value of $\gamma$.}
Although $\gamma$ does not improve aggregate calibration, within-family domain analysis reveals Social Sciences showing the strongest directional signal ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, posterior $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$; Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma}, Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_gamma_table}). ContamCal's value lies in this decomposition: it quantifies \textit{why} raw accuracy is robust (the effect is too small to distort rankings) rather than improving MAE.

\paragraph{Oracle ablation.}
To test whether non-significant pooled $\gamma$ reflects poor signal quality or weak item-level effects, we replace RRF scores with ground-truth binary labels (AUC$=$1.0). Even with perfect detection, pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant under the linear additive specification: MMLU $\gamma = -0.012$ (CI $[-0.145, 0.119]$, PPP$=$0.41). One explanation is effect heterogeneity: injection inflates some items while disrupting others (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), and a shared $\gamma$ averages over opposing directions. Threshold-type misspecification is ruled out by simulation: the linear specification overestimates rather than attenuates $\gamma$ under step-function DGPs (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold_dgp}). This also confirms that filtering is not circular: selected items need not produce a significant pooled $\gamma$.

\paragraph{Comparison to baselines.}
B6-DCR \citep{xu2025dcr} produces dramatically higher MAE (0.108), systematically overcorrecting a near-zero effect. B5-Emperor \citep{sun2025emperor} matches raw accuracy on MAE. B1b-Remove\&Rescore degrades MAE by removing informative items.

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

\paragraph{Min-K\%++ directional reversal.}
Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at the 0.5B scale: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005 and $d = -1.98$ at d100 relative to d000). A practitioner applying Min-K\%++ at this scale would conclude that contaminated items are \textit{less} likely to be memorized. At 1.5B, discrimination turns mildly positive ($d \approx +0.3$); at 3B, moderately positive ($d \approx +0.7$). We hypothesize that SFT injection at 0.5B disrupts the token-level surprisal landscape so severely that contaminated items (overfit at the response level) paradoxically show reduced token-level fluency. This causal explanation is post-hoc; alternative explanations include position bias confound at 0.5B and near-full-rank LoRA adaptation disrupting the pre-trained loss landscape.

\paragraph{Signal reliability across scales.}
Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap} summarizes detection AUC across signal-scale-dosage combinations. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at d005 and above for all scales. S4 (Self-Critique) performs near-random at 0.5B and reaches marginal discrimination at 1.5B+. At 0.5B, S2 and S4 contribute noise, which reduces RRF's signal quality advantage. However, multi-signal fusion remains essential for exposure variance: S1-only item selection produces compressed exposure distributions that prevent IRT convergence (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}).

Cross-family analysis with Llama-3.2-3B confirms that RRF exposure scores generalize across architectures, though pooled $\gamma$ loses diagnostic specificity (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_signal_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Detection signal AUC across scale, dosage, and signal type. S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $>0.95$ at all scales; S2 (Min-K\%++) reverses at 0.5B; S4 (Self-Critique) is near-random below 1.5B.}
\label{fig:signal_heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Identifiability Boundaries}
\label{sec:identifiability}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_identifiability_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{$\gamma$ recovery as a function of signal AUC and ensemble size $J$. Bias $<0.05$ and 95\% CI coverage $\geq90\%$ require $J\geq50$ at AUC$=$0.55, but only $J\geq16$ at AUC$\geq$0.90.}
\label{fig:identifiability_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:identifiability_heatmap} presents bias and CI coverage across 200 simulation runs. Recovery succeeds (bias $< 0.05$, 95\% CI coverage $\geq 90\%$) at AUC$=$0.55 with $J \geq 50$; CI width scales as $J^{-0.48}$. Current practice operates at $J = 8$--$30$ \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains,lalor_2024_irt_nlp}, well below this threshold. When AUC $\geq 0.90$ (as S1 achieves), $J = 16$ suffices. Our domain-level results ($J = 13$) confirm: calibration via $\theta$ works, but per-domain $\gamma$ remains exploratory (Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Exploratory Domain Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_domain_gamma.pdf}
\caption{Per-domain $\gamma_d$ estimates with 95\% CIs ($J$=13 Qwen2.5). STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Humanities and Social Sciences are negative. At $J$=16, Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$ survives Bonferroni correction (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}). These patterns are family-specific (\S\ref{sec:cross_family_validation}).}
\label{fig:domain_gamma}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Within-family directional heterogeneity.}
Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma} ($J=13$) and Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} (Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_gamma_table}) present per-domain $\gamma_d$ estimates for Qwen2.5 (0.5B excluded per \S\ref{sec:injection}). At $J=16$, STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative. Social Sciences shows the strongest directional signal ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, posterior $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722), crossing the Bonferroni-corrected threshold of 99.375\% for eight independent tests. This is a suggestive finding that requires higher-powered replication with $J \geq 50$ models. Prior sensitivity analysis confirms this signal is data-dominated rather than prior-dominated ($\hat{\gamma}_d$ varies only 1.2$\times$ across priors vs.\ 2.6$\times$ for the pooled estimate; Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity}). ARC-Challenge model fit is marginal at all tested $J$ (PPP $< 0.05$), which prevents reliable domain-level estimation on this benchmark. The pooled MMLU $\gamma_{\text{pooled}} = +0.11$ (CI $[-0.40, +0.63]$) is non-significant, as expected given opposing domain signs. These directional patterns are family-specific (\S\ref{sec:cross_family_validation}).

Dose-response analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}) shows scale-dependent effects: at 1.5B and 3B, accuracy increases with dosage, while at 0.5B, accuracy \textit{decreases} (0.41 $\to$ 0.27 on MMLU) as position bias overwhelms any memorization benefit (Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response_details}).

